WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.770
Have you ever opened a message and started writing

2
00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:05.850
a reply before you've even finished reading it? Let's

3
00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:08.690
walk through a moment like that. You're helping update

4
00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:12.230
a service notice, and a partner writes, we raised

5
00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:15.310
this concern last month. Why is it still here?

6
00:00:16.011 --> 00:00:19.551
I'd probably feel defensive. We've been working hard on

7
00:00:19.571 --> 00:00:22.111
that notice, and now it sounds as though none

8
00:00:22.131 --> 00:00:25.291
of that effort counts. My first instinct might be

9
00:00:25.311 --> 00:00:27.071
to explain how busy we've been.

10
00:00:27.804 --> 00:00:31.604
That's a very understandable reaction. But before you send

11
00:00:31.624 --> 00:00:34.004
it, what would help you find out whether something

12
00:00:34.024 --> 00:00:34.964
really was missed?

13
00:00:35.583 --> 00:00:38.123
I'd put the reply aside for a moment and

14
00:00:38.143 --> 00:00:41.423
open the comments. Was the concern about this version?

15
00:00:41.843 --> 00:00:45.043
Did we respond? I can feel frustrated and still

16
00:00:45.063 --> 00:00:47.723
check. The feeling doesn't have to make the decision

17
00:00:47.743 --> 00:00:48.303
for me.

18
00:00:49.263 --> 00:00:53.343
That's a useful starting point for emotional intelligence: noticing

19
00:00:53.363 --> 00:00:55.803
what you're feeling, then choosing what to do with

20
00:00:55.823 --> 00:00:58.163
it. You don't have to pretend to be calm.

21
00:00:58.723 --> 00:01:00.763
You need enough room to respond thoughtfully.

22
00:01:01.289 --> 00:01:04.449
So, the message is what happened. Frustration is what

23
00:01:04.469 --> 00:01:07.149
I'm feeling. They don't appreciate our work is my

24
00:01:07.169 --> 00:01:10.649
interpretation. Those are three different things, even though they

25
00:01:10.669 --> 00:01:11.529
can arrive together.

26
00:01:12.304 --> 00:01:15.564
Exactly. And it helps to ask, what else could

27
00:01:15.584 --> 00:01:18.724
explain this? The partner might be worried that someone

28
00:01:18.744 --> 00:01:21.164
will receive a notice and have no idea whom

29
00:01:21.184 --> 00:01:24.384
to call. That's worth checking, even if the message

30
00:01:24.404 --> 00:01:25.164
feels sharp.

31
00:01:26.112 --> 00:01:29.812
What would you actually write back? I don't want

32
00:01:29.832 --> 00:01:32.112
to leave them waiting while I sort out my

33
00:01:32.132 --> 00:01:32.692
reaction.

34
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:37.080
Something simple: thanks for bringing this back to us.

35
00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:40.780
I'm checking your comments against the current notice. I'll

36
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.700
get back to you this afternoon with what I

37
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.040
find. Choose a time you can meet.

38
00:01:45.626 --> 00:01:48.647
That sounds like something I could say. It acknowledges

39
00:01:48.667 --> 00:01:51.886
the concern without promising a decision I can't make.

40
00:01:52.267 --> 00:01:54.507
And if I need longer, I should tell them

41
00:01:54.527 --> 00:01:56.527
before that afternoon commitment passes.

42
00:01:57.647 --> 00:02:01.107
Now you find the comment. The notice tells people

43
00:02:01.127 --> 00:02:04.667
to contact their worker, but doesn't explain what to

44
00:02:04.687 --> 00:02:07.407
do if they don't know who that is. Where

45
00:02:07.427 --> 00:02:08.487
would you go from there?

46
00:02:09.312 --> 00:02:12.432
I'd check the right contact with the responsible team,

47
00:02:12.772 --> 00:02:15.452
then take the proposed correction to whoever approves the

48
00:02:15.472 --> 00:02:18.652
notice. I'd also tell the partner, I can see

49
00:02:18.672 --> 00:02:21.252
why that instruction could leave someone unsure where to

50
00:02:21.272 --> 00:02:21.692
turn.

51
00:02:22.407 --> 00:02:25.847
That acknowledgment matters. You aren't claiming to know exactly

52
00:02:25.867 --> 00:02:29.087
how someone feels. You're showing that you understand the

53
00:02:29.107 --> 00:02:30.347
problem they're trying to solve.

54
00:02:31.657 --> 00:02:34.417
And I'd keep the promise small enough to deliver.

55
00:02:34.917 --> 00:02:37.497
I can bring the concern forward and explain the

56
00:02:37.517 --> 00:02:41.077
decision. I can't promise that every suggestion will be

57
00:02:41.097 --> 00:02:43.497
accepted or that a release will be delayed.

58
00:02:44.116 --> 00:02:46.976
Suppose you discover this has happened more than once.

59
00:02:46.996 --> 00:02:49.256
Would a better reply be enough?

60
00:02:49.752 --> 00:02:52.932
Probably not. We'd need a reliable place for comments,

61
00:02:53.372 --> 00:02:55.552
someone responsible for reviewing them, and a way to

62
00:02:55.572 --> 00:02:58.372
explain the result. A supervisor can help clear up

63
00:02:58.392 --> 00:03:01.652
that responsibility. Staff shouldn't have to solve every repeated

64
00:03:01.672 --> 00:03:03.232
problem through personal patience.

65
00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:06.840
Let's give listeners a moment to try this. Think

66
00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:09.460
about a difficult message, or use the notice we've

67
00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:13.800
just discussed. What happened? What are you assuming? What's

68
00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:15.800
one question you could check before replying?

69
00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:20.390
Then write two sentences: acknowledge the concern, and say

70
00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:22.890
what you'll do next. Keep the text in front

71
00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:25.690
of you if that helps. You can pause here

72
00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:26.790
and take your time.

73
00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:31.530
Afterward, check the result. Did the person receive an

74
00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:35.210
answer? Was the instruction clearer? Did the missed review

75
00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:38.330
get repaired? A friendly exchange is valuable, and the

76
00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:40.210
follow-through is what makes the help dependable.

77
00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:43.940
You don't need a perfect response every time. Notice

78
00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:47.260
the moment, check your interpretation, and take one useful

79
00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.920
next step. That's something you can practice in an

80
00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:50.560
ordinary workday.
